heart disease is the number one killer  of women but a new survey in the Journal  of the American College of Cardiology  found it is not a top priority for women  or their doctors cardiovascular disease  causes 400,000 deaths a year and that is  more than all cancers combined think  about that for a second 45% of women are  unaware of the danger our dr. Tara  Narula is a cardiologist at the  northwell health and she joins us this  morning at the table to discuss because  this Tara is important in its big a lot  of women say that they want to lose  weight before they go to the doctor they  say they'd much rather do their taxes  and go to their doctor how can this be  when heart disease is so important and  so prevalent for women it is killing one  woman every 80 seconds and yet women  don't recognize that this is their  biggest threat so one of the barriers is  that it's just not real for a lot of  women they say I'm in shape I'm too  young I don't have a lot of risk factors  in fact in this survey many said I don't  really know any woman who's had heart  disease so it needs to become more of a  real threat to them needs to become  personalized that's one barrier another  barrier is certainly the social stigma  that you mentioned which is a lot of  women feel that if they talk about heart  disease with their doctor they might be  looked down upon in a sense that will be  judged they'll be judged that they don't  eat right that they don't exercise and  so in fact 45% of women in the survey  said they wanted to lose a few pounds  before they went to the doctor so they  could be their best self and that sounds  crazy but that's how these women are  thinking about it other issues women  tend to downplay their symptoms I can't  tell you how many times women tell me  well it's just pret it's just stress  it's just stress I'm sure or they don't  recognize their risk factors or symptoms  you know 40% of women who have a heart  attack never had chest pain they can  present atypically with nausea anxiety  jaw pain so it's important that we make  that awareness known so how often should  we be screened for heart issues so this  should be a conversation that happens at  every visit in fact the American Heart  Association recommends the women start  having what they call a well woman visit  annually where you talk about risk  factors you get screened one of the most  interesting things about this survey is  that doctors primary cares and  cardiologists felt unprepared to deal  with women and cardiovascular health and  that's a problem in fact they weren't  prioritizing it they were prioritizing  in this survey weight issues and breast  health not cardiovascular disease tell  why is that a problem at this point  we've heard about this for years the  doctors tend to look at men for heart  disease but not women why haven't  doctors gotten the message that's a  great question I mean it has been under  researched underfunded under studied in  general and I think one of the big  issues of this survey is to raise  awareness and doctors that they need to  be screening women every single visit  they need to be talking about this not  waiting for their patients to bring it  up because 71% of the women in the study  waited for their doctor to bring it up  and if the doctor didn't bring it up it  didn't get discussed so it needs to be a  discussion happening at every visit  women can have respecters that are  particular to them to that they don't  often think about depression anxiety  autoimmune diseases like rubus lupus  rheumatoid arthritis pregnancy  complications so important to understand  the whole picture and bottom line and  overwhelming majority of heart disease  is preventable it is that is the good  news thank you always good to have you  here dr. taryn urilla thank you
 

